1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,960
Failing test output from the bike-share
CI: Look at this code for a moment. The
controller class has @RestController. The
starter-web is on the classpath. The slice
test even looks right.

2
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:29,000
Root cause:
@SpringBootApplication(scanBasePackages =
"com.bikeshare.service") on the main class
— written during a "only scan services"
cleanup — excludes com.bikeshare.api.web
where controllers live.

3
00:00:29,119 --> 00:00:40,799
Auto-configuration still runs. Component
scan does not see the controllers. Fusion
hid the knob that was turned.
@SpringBootApplication is a composed
annotation.

4
00:00:40,919 --> 00:00:51,999
It combines @SpringBootConfiguration (a
@Configuration specialization for the
application), @EnableAutoConfiguration,
and @ComponentScan with defaults.

5
00:00:52,119 --> 00:01:00,559
One annotation on the main class enables
Java config of the app itself, Boot auto-
config, and scanning of the main class
package and subpackages.

6
00:01:00,679 --> 00:01:13,919
When you override one attribute, you are
not "tweaking Boot" in the abstract — you
are changing one of those three meta-
annotations' behavior. Look at this java
for a moment. Walk the happy path.

7
00:01:14,039 --> 00:01:33,039
BikeShareApi lives in com.bikeshare.
Default @ComponentScan uses that package
as base, so
com.bikeshare.api.web.StationController is
a candidate, becomes a bean, and MVC maps
GET /api/stations/{id}.

8
00:01:33,159 --> 00:01:47,000
SpringApplication.run(BikeShareApi.class,
args) registers that class as a
configuration source:
@SpringBootConfiguration makes it a
configuration class;
@EnableAutoConfiguration imports auto-
config;

9
00:01:47,119 --> 00:01:50,799
scan finds the controller and
StationDirectory under service packages.

10
00:01:50,919 --> 00:02:04,919
Constructor injection on the controller is
ordinary DI once the bean exists. Runtime
of the broken knob. scanBasePackages =
"com.bikeshare.service" replaces the
default base package list.

11
00:02:05,039 --> 00:02:16,280
Only types under com.bikeshare.service are
scanned. Auto-configuration still creates
DispatcherServlet, handler mappings
infrastructure, embedded Tomcat.

12
00:02:16,399 --> 00:02:27,559
Zero StationController beans → no request
handler for /api/stations/42 → MockMvc
404. Actuator health may still be UP.

13
00:02:27,679 --> 00:02:34,079
That split — infrastructure up,
application handlers missing — is the
characteristic symptom of scan-base
mistakes.

14
00:02:34,199 --> 00:02:45,319
Putting the main class in a deliberately
chosen root package is a structural
decision; narrowing scan without moving
controllers is how empty handler maps look
"healthy" while HTTP dies.

15
00:02:45,439 --> 00:02:56,759
Failure mode variants: main class in
com.bikeshare.Bootstrap under a deep
package while controllers sit in
com.bikeshare.api — default scan never
climbs *up* to siblings. Symptom same 404.

16
00:02:56,879 --> 00:03:10,000
Or @SpringBootApplication(exclude =
DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
misunderstood as "exclude scanning" —
different attribute, different meta-
annotation.

17
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:28,199
Or a @WebMvcTest(StationController.class)
that does not use the right @Import /
@ContextConfiguration and fails for test-
slice reasons unrelated to production scan
— check whether production beans endpoint
lists the cont

18
00:03:28,319 --> 00:03:30,719
roller before blaming MockMvc.

19
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:41,039
Trade-offs. Composition keeps main classes
one line and onboarding short; it also
hides three mechanisms behind one name, so
debugging requires unpacking the
composition.

20
00:03:41,159 --> 00:03:53,079
Explicit @ComponentScan /
@EnableAutoConfiguration on a
@Configuration class is more verbose and
clearer for unusual layouts (multiple
modules, selective auto-config).

21
00:03:53,199 --> 00:04:03,799
Prefer moving the main class to the right
package over maintaining long
scanBasePackages arrays that drift as
modules grow. Unpack the composition when
debugging the next 404.

22
00:04:03,919 --> 00:04:14,959
Open @SpringBootApplication's declaration:
you will see @SpringBootConfiguration,
@EnableAutoConfiguration, and
@ComponentScan as meta-annotations.

23
00:04:15,079 --> 00:04:24,479
Attributes like scanBasePackages, exclude,
and excludeName are @AliasFor mappings
onto those meta-annotations — not
mysterious Boot-only flags.

24
00:04:24,599 --> 00:04:31,079
When CI says bean count zero for a
controller, ask which of the three
composed behaviors you changed.

25
00:04:31,199 --> 00:04:38,839
Auto-config exclusion never removes a
@RestController from the scan; scan
narrowing never disables DataSource auto-
config.

26
00:04:38,959 --> 00:04:44,759
Separating those mental models turns a
fused annotation back into three levers
you can reason about.

27
00:04:44,879 --> 00:04:54,959
Misconception unique to
@SpringBootApplication: "It only enables
auto-configuration; scanning is separate
and optional." Scanning is included.

28
00:04:55,079 --> 00:05:02,359
Disabling or narrowing it without moving
controllers is how empty contexts look
healthy while HTTP dies. Controllers map
again.

29
00:05:02,479 --> 00:05:10,719
Configuration is still a mess of
@Value("${freight.rate.base}") scattered
across constructors in a related rates
module.

30
00:05:10,839 --> 00:05:18,279
Typed binding — @ConfigurationProperties —
is the next cleanup that keeps Environment
values honest as a group.
